<BaseLayout title="Seattle, the Olympics and Portland — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Two trips to the Pacific Northwest in the summer of 2023: Seattle from the 73rd floor, a one-day loop around the Olympic Peninsula, a sunset sail into Shilshole Bay, and a weekend in Portland with roses and Mt. Hood.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · June–July 2023</p>
  <h1>Seattle, the Olympics and Portland</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Seattle, the Olympic Peninsula and a sunset sail in June; Portland in July.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>June 16–24 and July 20–23, 2023</strong><br />
    June: Seattle → the Edmonds–Kingston ferry → Port Angeles → Lake Crescent → US 101 → Aberdeen → Seattle ·
    a sunset sail into Shilshole Bay · Bellevue<br />
    July: Kirkland → Portland → back to Seattle over the I-90 floating bridge<br />
    Two rental cars · one ferry · one sailboat · two volcanoes, both in July
  </div>

  <p>
    In the summer of 2023 I had an internship in Redmond, near Seattle, and my wife flew up twice, a month apart.
    The June half of this post covers nine days, from a Friday to the following Saturday, and my wife came for the first
    weekend of it: we were together from Friday night, June 16th, to Monday, June 19th, and she flew home that Monday
    evening. The rest of June below is just me. In July we both came back for a weekend, Thursday night to Sunday,
    and drove down to Portland and back.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with my other trip posts, everything here comes from the camera rolls, mine and my wife's: where each photo
    was taken and when. Where neither phone recorded anything, I've left the gap alone. Between Monday, June 19th, and
    Friday, June 23rd, for example, there is nothing at all.
  </p>

  <h2 id="june">June: Seattle and the Olympic Peninsula</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-june.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-june.webp'} alt="Map of western Washington: Seattle stops at the University of Washington and the Sky View Observatory, a loop by ferry from Edmonds to Ediz Hook, Lake Crescent, US 101 near the coast and Aberdeen, then Shilshole Bay and Bellevue." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The June stops. The big loop is Sunday, June 18th.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    I landed on Friday evening, June 16th, and at 8:45 pm I was in the rental garage at Sea-Tac walking around a
    black Tesla Model 3, taking the usual photos of every side before driving off. My wife came on a later flight
    from LAX. Just before one in the morning we were on I-5 with downtown lit up ahead, and at 1:34
    am she was photographing a late meal in the Chinatown–International District: roast duck, fried rice and something
    fried with a dipping sauce.
  </p>

  <h3>Saturday: the university, and the city from above</h3>
  <p>
    Saturday was gray. We had pizza for lunch in Kirkland, on the east side of Lake Washington, and then drove
    across into Seattle. At 3:40 pm we were walking through the <strong>University of Washington</strong>. My wife's
    photos follow the walk: brick halls under a low sky, George Washington's statue, the giant old trees on the
    lawns, and <strong>Suzzallo Library</strong>, the Gothic library with its row of tall arched windows.
    The campus also has a section in <a href="/misc/campuses">the campuses post</a>. At 4:11 we were on University Way,
    "the Ave", in line for drinks.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'uw-suzzallo.webp'} alt="The long Gothic facade of Suzzallo Library at the University of Washington, with tall arched windows and pinnacles, under a gray sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Suzzallo Library, 3:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At 6:18 we drove south over the Ship Canal Bridge on I-5. From the bridge, downtown stood across Lake Union in a
    flat light, with the Space Needle off to one side.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lake-union-skyline.webp'} alt="The downtown Seattle skyline across Lake Union under a gray sky, seen from a freeway bridge with a lamppost and railing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown across Lake Union, from the Ship Canal Bridge, 6:18 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then we went up. From 6:54 to 7:42 pm we were on the <strong>Sky View Observatory</strong> on the 73rd floor of
    the Columbia Center, and between the two of us we took dozens of photos up there. The best of mine,
    the light breaking through over Puget Sound and the stadium packed for an event, are already in
    <a href="/misc/views-from-above#seattle">Views from Up High</a>. Here are three of my wife's. Looking north, the
    Space Needle looked small among the towers, with Lake Union and the hills beyond it. Straight down, at 7:25, a
    ferry was tied up at the terminal on the waterfront, with lines of cars waiting to board.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sky-view-needle.webp'} alt="Looking down from a skyscraper on downtown Seattle towers, with the Space Needle among them and Lake Union and the hills under clouds beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Space Needle from the 73rd floor, 7:14 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sky-view-colman-dock.webp'} alt="A white ferry docked at a terminal seen from directly above, with rows of cars in the holding lanes below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A ferry at the terminal, 7:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Looking west, another ferry was crossing the Sound toward Bainbridge Island, leaving a long wake on gray water.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sky-view-ferry.webp'} alt="A ferry and a smaller boat crossing gray water with long wakes, toward a forested island under a band of pale light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A ferry heading out across Puget Sound, 7:16 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner was in Bellevue at 8:21: a dry pot, half of it red broth and half a heap of dried chilies, with a
    bottle of beer next to it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'bellevue-dry-pot.webp'} alt="A divided metal pot with red broth on one side and a heap of dried red chilies on the other, with a beer bottle beside it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dinner in Bellevue, 8:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Sunday: around the Olympic Peninsula in a day</h3>
  <p>
    Sunday was the long day: a loop around the Olympic Peninsula, the big block of mountains and forest west of
    Seattle, across Puget Sound. At 1:30 pm we
    were at the ferry dock in <strong>Edmonds</strong>, north of Seattle, and at 2:10 the boat brought us into
    <strong>Kingston</strong> on the other side. From there the road runs northwest to the Strait of Juan de Fuca.
  </p>
  <p>
    By four we were in <strong>Port Angeles</strong>, out on <strong>Ediz Hook</strong>, the long spit that curls around
    the harbor. Across the water the town sat at the foot of the Olympic Mountains, and the mountains were wearing
    a thick layer of cloud. On the other side of the spit, the strait stretched away to the north, and a gull stood
    on top of an old piling stuck into the rocks. My wife photographed the same gull a few seconds after me. Our selfies
    from there stay in the family album.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'port-angeles-mountains.webp'} alt="Port Angeles at the foot of dark mountains capped with heavy gray cloud, seen across calm water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Port Angeles and the Olympics, 4:14 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ediz-hook-gull.webp'} alt="A gull standing on top of a leaning weathered piling on a rocky shore, with open water and scattered clouds behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A gull on Ediz Hook, 4:16 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    West of Port Angeles, US 101 runs along the south shore of <strong>Lake Crescent</strong>. At 4:55 we stopped by the
    guardrail. The lake is long and narrow and sits between steep mountains covered in dark forest, and that afternoon
    it lay under a gray sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lake-crescent.webp'} alt="A steep, densely forested mountainside dropping into a lake under a gray sky, with a rusty guardrail in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lake Crescent, 4:55 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    After that the highway turns south down the west side of the peninsula. My wife's video at 6:22 pm, taken
    near the coast, is of US 101 running straight through tall forest, with the evening sun coming through the trees
    and one car far ahead.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'us101-forest.webp'} alt="A two-lane highway running straight between tall conifers lit by low sun, with one car far ahead." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>US 101 near the coast, 6:22 pm (a still from a video).</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At 7:58 we reached <strong>Aberdeen</strong>, at the southern end of the loop. The sign that greets you there is
    mounted high up on the steel tower of an old lift bridge: "Welcome to Aberdeen, 'Gateway to the Olympics'". I
    photographed it from every angle under a big sky of clouds.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'aberdeen-sign.webp'} alt="A purple 'Welcome to Aberdeen, Gateway to the Olympics' sign on top of a steel bridge tower, against blue sky and white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The welcome sign in Aberdeen, 7:58 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From Aberdeen the road goes east to Olympia and then up I-5. At 9:47 pm we were driving back into Seattle at dusk,
    and at 10:21 we had a late dinner in north Seattle: a whole table of skewers, grilled oysters, fried noodles
    and more.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'skewers.webp'} alt="A restaurant table covered with plates of grilled skewers, grilled oysters, fried noodles and other dishes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Late dinner, 10:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Monday</h3>
  <p>
    On Monday afternoon my wife's photos are from downtown Bellevue: two drinks at 3:38 pm and a crêpe at 5:03.
    That evening she flew home to Los Angeles. My own phone has nothing from Monday to Thursday.
  </p>

  <h3>Friday: a sunset sail</h3>
  <p>
    The next photos are mine, alone, on Friday evening, June 23rd. At 7:33 pm I
    was on a sailboat off <strong>Bainbridge Island</strong>, out on open water. Over the next hour the boat sailed back
    across Puget Sound toward Seattle while the sun went down behind the Olympic Mountains.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sail-sunset.webp'} alt="The sun low over Puget Sound, with a bright path of light on the water and the faint outline of mountains on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sun over the Sound, 8:26 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sail-rigging.webp'} alt="An orange sun setting over dark mountains and calm water, seen past the railing and fittings of a sailboat." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset from the boat, 8:51 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    At 8:51 the sun touched the mountains and turned the sky orange. By nine the boat was coming into
    <strong>Shilshole Bay Marina</strong> in Ballard. The water inside the breakwater was perfectly still, and the
    masts of the moored boats stood in it twice, once in the air and once upside down, under an
    orange-and-blue sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'shilshole-masts.webp'} alt="Rows of sailboat masts in a marina reflected in perfectly calm water, under an orange and blue sunset sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Shilshole Bay Marina, 9:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Saturday in Bellevue</h3>
  <p>
    Saturday, June 24th, was sunny, and I spent it in downtown Bellevue: a long line outside a milk-tea shop,
    a mall and an Apple Store, the "#BVUE" letters, and a row of rainbow-painted Pride letters. A set of
    white, tree-shaped canopies stood among the towers. A few minutes later I was in <strong>Bellevue Downtown
    Park</strong>, a big lawn with a long waterfall along one side, with ducks resting at its foot.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bellevue-canopies.webp'} alt="White sculptural canopies shaped like branching trees in front of glass residential towers, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Canopies in downtown Bellevue, 3:57 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bellevue-waterfall.webp'} alt="A long, wide waterfall spilling over a wall into a green pool, with ducks along the edge and trees above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The waterfall in Bellevue Downtown Park, 4:03 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    It ended with an ice-cream cone in the afternoon and hot pot and soft-serve in the evening. My photos stop at
    9:27 pm that Saturday. The flight home is not in the camera roll.
  </p>

  <h2 id="july">July: a weekend in Portland</h2>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-july.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-july.webp'} alt="Map of western Washington and Oregon: Juanita Beach in Kirkland, the drive down I-5 across the Interstate Bridge to downtown Portland and the Rose Test Garden, and back north to the I-90 floating bridge in Seattle." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The July weekend: Kirkland, down to Portland, and back.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    A month later we were back. Both of us flew up from Los Angeles on Thursday, July 20th, on separate flights. I
    picked up a rental car, a black Nissan Rogue this time, at Sea-Tac at 7:43 pm, and my wife flew up from LAX that
    night.
  </p>
  <p>
    Friday was the opposite of June: blue sky and sun. In the afternoon my wife's photos are from
    <strong>Juanita Beach</strong> in Kirkland, on Lake Washington: a sandy beach, a long wooden pier, people wading,
    and ducks in the shallows. Then came two bubble teas from RareTea in Kirkland, and we set off for Portland.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'juanita-beach.webp'} alt="A long wooden pier running out into the calm blue water of a lake from a sandy beach, with ducks in the shallows and a wooded shore beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Juanita Beach, Kirkland, 2:44 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-portland.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-portland.webp'} alt="Map of Portland: dinner downtown, Council Crest, a church downtown, Powell's City of Books, the Rose Test Garden, the Mt. Hood viewpoint in the West Hills, and dinner on 12th Avenue." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Portland, Friday night and Saturday.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    At 8:11 pm we crossed the Columbia River on the Interstate Bridge, a long green steel truss, and were in Oregon.
    We had dinner downtown at 8:23, in a restaurant lit pink and purple. Then we drove up to
    <strong>Council Crest</strong>, the high point in the hills southwest of downtown. At 9:52 the last blue was
    fading over the hills, and the city lights were spread out below the dark trees.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'council-crest.webp'} alt="Silhouettes of fir trees against a deep blue dusk sky, with the lights of Portland spread out below and a line of hills on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Portland from Council Crest, 9:52 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Back downtown at 10:17, we walked past a dark stone Gothic church with a tall spire. The lights were on inside, and its big stained-glass window glowed
    in amber, red and blue against the night.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rose-window.webp'} alt="A large stained-glass window in amber, gold, red and blue, lit from inside and seen at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The big window, 10:17 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'church-night.webp'} alt="A dark stone Gothic church with a lit spire and glowing stained-glass windows, seen from below at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The church from the street, 10:17 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Saturday: books, roses and a volcano</h3>
  <p>
    On Saturday morning, from the window of our hotel downtown, the snow-covered top of <strong>Mt. Hood</strong>
    showed between two buildings, far to the east. It would come back in the afternoon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hotel-view-hood.webp'} alt="Rooftops of downtown Portland from a high window, with a glass tower on the left and the snowy cone of Mt. Hood on the horizon between buildings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mt. Hood from the hotel window, 11:03 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    After brunch we went to <strong>Powell's City of Books</strong>, which fills a whole city block on Burnside.
    At 1:03 pm the marquee was announcing author events and a crowd was waiting at the corner. My wife's photos for the next
    hour are from the shops nearby.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'powells.webp'} alt="The corner entrance of Powell's Books with its red and cream 'Used &amp; New Books' marquee, people waiting at the crosswalk, and apartment towers behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Powell's City of Books, 1:03 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At 3:37 we were in Washington Park, at the <strong>International Rose Test Garden</strong>. The garden is a set of
    terraces on a hillside, with beds of roses in every color between lawns and hedges, and it was in full
    bloom. Most of the photos we took there are of my wife among the roses, and they stay in the family album.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rose-garden.webp'} alt="Terraced beds of pink, white and coral roses in full bloom, with hedges and tall trees behind under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Rose Test Garden, 3:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The rest are of the flowers themselves.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rose-red.webp'} alt="Close-up of a single deep red rose in full bloom among green leaves." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A red rose, 3:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rose-orange.webp'} alt="A cluster of peach, orange and cream roses in sunlight." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Peach and cream, 3:55 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From the garden we drove a little higher into the West Hills, to a bend in the road below Pittock Mansion. At 4:20
    the whole of downtown Portland was laid out below the trees, and behind it, standing alone above the hills,
    was Mt. Hood. My own photo from that minute is in
    <a href="/misc/views-from-above#portland">Views from Up High</a>, next to one from the same spot two and a half years
    later. This is my wife's, zoomed in a little.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mt-hood-downtown.webp'} alt="Downtown Portland's towers below forested hills, with the snow-covered cone of Mt. Hood rising above the horizon under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Portland and Mt. Hood, 4:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner that night, at 8:05, was back downtown on 12th Avenue: crawfish étouffée, with a whole red crawfish sitting
    on the rice, and a plate of fried bites with two dipping sauces.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'etouffee.webp'} alt="A plate of crawfish étouffée with a mound of rice in the middle, topped by a whole red crawfish." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Crawfish étouffée, 8:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Sunday: back north</h3>
  <p>
    There are no photos of the drive back, but by midday on Sunday we were back on the Seattle side. That evening my
    wife flew home from Sea-Tac; her last photos of the trip are at the airport at 7:42 pm. Eleven minutes later I was
    crossing Lake Washington on the <strong>I-90 floating bridge</strong>, and there, to the south, was the other
    volcano: <strong>Mount Rainier</strong>, white above a wooded shore, in a clear evening sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rainier-i90.webp'} alt="Mount Rainier's snowy peak rising above a forested island shore across the blue water of Lake Washington, under a pale clear sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mount Rainier from the I-90 floating bridge, July 23, 7:53 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Two trips, then, a month apart: one gray and one blue. June has the city from the 73rd floor, the peninsula in a
    day and a sail into a marina at sunset. July has Portland's books and roses, and two volcanoes in two days.
  </p>
